fact WorkItem(id: "WI-864", description: some(value: "WI-857 FOLLOW-UP (perf, independent) — the instance-resolution path got ~5x hotter and one of its primitives is an O(|symbols|) SCAN. WI-857 made a dictionary bundle the SPEC's requires chain as well as the provider's, so resolve_inner now walks the spec chain too: resolving Ordered[T = Int64] went from 1 node to 5, and the prelude diamond (Ordered requires Eq + PartialOrd, both requiring PartialEq) resolves PartialEq TWICE. MEASURED end-to-end: rustland/scripts/test.sh 219s -> 235s (+7%). TWO costs, one path. (1) collect_provides_candidates calls kb.type_params_of_sort(goal.spec_sort) per goal, and that (kb/mod.rs) does a format! plus a FULL SCAN of by_qualified_name with a starts_with per entry, then clones a Vec<String> — so per-goal cost is O(|symbols|) and the goal count just went up ~5x. Memoize it on a RefCell<HashMap<Symbol, Rc<Vec<String>>>>: the sort_param_pairs_cache (kb/mod.rs, used by typing.rs) is the SAME walk already cached this way, so the pattern and its invalidation are established. This is the only cost here with a superlinear factor and it is independent of WI-857 — it was always O(|symbols|), WI-857 only amplified it. (2) dict_sub_goals builds a SmallVec<[(String, TermId); 2]> per resolved goal (resolve_sym(k).to_string()) to feed provider_requires_subgoals' short-name-keyed sigma; its only consumer, map_requires_name, compares n == kb.resolve_sym(s), so keying by Symbol and comparing at the point of use preserves the documented contract with ZERO allocation. Touches check_provider_requires' call site, so it is a signature change, not a local edit. NOTE the sigma-by-short-name keying is pre-existing documented debt (the loader does not record the cross-param binding when the names differ) — WI-857 moved it from once-per-load onto a per-dispatch path, so the debt is now hotter but not new. OPTIONAL THIRD: a per-resolve memo threaded alongside resolve_inner's stack, keyed on (SortGoal, local_provider), kills the diamond duplication exactly — local_provider MUST be in the key, because WI-857's locality rule made a sub-goal's outcome depend on it. resolve_bridge_requirements calls the UNCACHED resolve per requires slot on the per-dispatch value-directed path with a constant empty scope, so that regime is soundly cacheable. ACCEPTANCE: the memo(s) in place; suite wall-clock measured BEFORE and AFTER on the same machine and reported (the 219/235 numbers above are the baseline pair); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-17T07:06:19Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-864", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED, BUT NOT AS SPECIFIED — the ticket's two named costs are DEAD and the real one is a third it does not mention. Instrumented before optimizing (counters/timers around every suspect, in-process over a stdlib+host-bindings load, min-of-K):\n\n  collect_provides_candidates      189 calls   43.9 ms   <-- the path the ticket is about\n    type_params_of_sort (cost 1)   189 calls    0.035 ms  <-- 0.03% of a ~110 ms release load\n    dict_sub_goals sigma (cost 2)  143 calls    0.012 ms  <-- 0.01%\n    match_candidate_against_goal  6332 calls   37.7 ms\n      dispatch_values_match       6310 calls   37.7 ms\n        types_lesseq              6310 calls   35.9 ms\n          sort_provides_admissibly 5944 calls  27.5 ms   <-- 63% of the whole path\n\nCOST (1) IS ALREADY FIXED. The ticket says `type_params_of_sort` \"does a format! plus a FULL SCAN of by_qualified_name with a starts_with per entry\". WI-954 deleted exactly that — it is now an owner-scope read (`type_param_syms_of`), and its own doc records the removal. Memoizing it would add a cache and an invalidation surface for 0.03% of a load. NOT DONE, and the ticket's \"the only cost here with a superlinear factor\" is false at HEAD.\n\nCOST (2) IS 0.012 ms. A signature change through `check_provider_requires`' call site for 0.01%. NOT DONE.\n\nTHE REAL COST is `sort_provides`' transitive walk, which the ticket never names. It re-materialized every reachable node's out-edges on EVERY call, and its cycle guard compared with `same_sort_canonical` — a `qualified_name_of` plus a `HashMap<String,_>` probe, i.e. a STRING HASH — once per visited entry per node. Per load: 6333 calls, 36 696 node visits, 28 805 edge re-decodes. Two fixes:\n\n * CANONICALIZE ONCE PER WALK. `same_sort_canonical(a,b)` is `a==b || canonical(a)==canonical(b)`, and `a==b` implies the right disjunct, so the predicate IS canonical equality — the walk can carry canonical symbols and compare them RAW. Identical verdicts, no hazard (`visited` is a local SmallVec).\n * MEMOIZE THE DECODED ADJACENCY as `ProvidesIndex::carrier_edges` (canonical carrier -> [(rid, canonical spec base)]), filled by `build_provides_index` from the SAME pass, using `provides_out_edges`' OWN decode so the memo is that function rather than a lookalike. It lives INSIDE `ProvidesIndex`, so every existing `provides_index = None` drops it and the one builder fills it — no new invalidation surface to audit (contrast WI-1112, which needed its producers enumerated). `is_rule_alive` stays on the read, because a frozen bucket cannot answer retraction.\n\nMEASURED, PAIRED, IN-PROCESS, BOTH ARMS ALTERNATING IN ONE PROCESS (a thread-local switch routed `sort_provides` down the HEAD walk), order rotated per pair, first pair dropped, release, idle box (load 0.17):\n\n                                   HEAD        WI-864\n  whole stdlib load (median of 13) 112.0 ms -> 99.5 ms   (-11.2%; WI-864 faster in 14 of 14 pairs)\n  collect_provides_candidates       40.5 ms -> 15.1 ms   (-63%)\n  sort_provides (6332 calls)        28.2 ms ->  5.5 ms   (-80%)\n  canonical_sym CALLS, whole load  465 995  -> 207 681   (-55%, a pure count, timing-free)\n  type_params_of_sort (cost 1)       0.035 ms -> 0.022   <-- the control: untouched, moves ~0\n\nSUITE WALL-CLOCK, alternating on this box (the instrument the ticket asks for, and the WEAKER one — see the WI-1111 note that a shell-sampled run spans 34x on one binary):\n  HEAD   371 s, 358 s      WI-864  355 s, 329 s     -> ~-6%, and both WI-864 samples beat both HEAD samples.\n\nITEM (3), the diamond memo: NOT DONE, and its proposed key is UNSOUND. Measured 23 repeated (goal, local_provider) pairs per load out of 157 `resolve_inner` entries — so ~1.8 ms at the new cost, not the headline. More importantly `resolve_inner`'s outcome also depends on `slot_pin` (WI-870 filters candidates by it), `rung` (WI-861 decides whether a default may answer) and `scope.available_requires` (decides `FromScope`); a memo keyed on (SortGoal, local_provider) alone would serve a tree computed under a different pin, rung or scope. If it is wanted, the key is four things, not two.\n\nTESTS — 4 driven, in `typing::wi864_provides_edges_tests`, and the CONTROLS were measured rather than predicted:\n * the memo answers what the live decode answers, per carrier, over EVERY sort the KB names (not the fixture's four).\n * a two-hop chain (`Bottom provides Mid provides Top`) resolves identically with and without the memo, with a negative and a direction check in the same run.\n * a retracted provision is dropped by the memo as by the decode. CONTROL FIRES: dropping `is_rule_alive` from the memo arm fails exactly this test and no other.\n * a non-canonical carrier is refused LOUDLY. CONTROL MEASURED: with the `debug_assert_eq!` removed the call answers `[]` — \"this carrier provides nothing\" — a wrong verdict, not a refusal, feeding dispatch and requires-coverage. That guard came from review.\n\nTWO CONTROLS I PREDICTED THAT DO NOT FIRE, recorded because predicting is not measuring: building the memo from the sibling `unwrap_spec_view` decode instead of `provides_spec_base_sym` changes nothing (the two agree on every provides fact in this corpus), and keying the memo on the RAW carrier keeps the WHOLE WORKSPACE green (4984) — every provides carrier here is already its own canonical form. Both ship on consistency, not on a failing case; said so at the site.\n\nREVIEW: /code-review high, two findings, both fixed here — the unenforced canonical precondition (now a `debug_assert_eq!` plus the driven test above) and a fixture that selected the fact to retract by carrier alone (now pins the spec side too, so a later pass emitting a second provision cannot make it retract an unrelated edge).\n\nFULL WORKSPACE GREEN: 4985 passed, 0 failed, 29 binaries, twice.", at: "2026-08-17T07:06:23Z")
